<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jafar Khan Zand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jafar Khan Zand was ruler of the Zand state from 1785 to 1789, a son of Sadeq Khan and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>. Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> during the civil wars that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s death, he lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and much of the north to the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader Agha Mohammad Khan. He was poisoned in 1789 in a palace conspiracy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jafar-khan-zand.jpg" alt="Jafar Khan Zand" width="217" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ursula Sims-Williams · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ja%60far_Khan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jafar Khan was the son of Sadegh Khan Zand, who had been removed from the throne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-murad-khan-zand">Ali Murad Khan</a>. When Ali Murad Khan invaded Shiraz in February 1781, he put Sadegh Khan and all his brothers to death; Jafar Khan alone was spared, because he had privately reached an agreement with him. In 1785, while Ali Murad Khan was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Jafar Khan took the chance to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. Ali Murad Khan marched to defend the city but died on 11 February at Murchakhur, a town near Isfahan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jafar Khan crowned himself king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> warlord Agha Mohammad Khan marched on the city at once. The men Jafar Khan sent to stop him withdrew at Qom without resistance, and a larger Zand army was defeated near Kashan. Jafar Khan fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, and Agha Mohammad Khan took Kashan and then Isfahan, where he found what was left of the Zand treasure and Jafar Khan's harem, and his troops looted the city.</p><p>The pattern repeated in the following years. In 1786, with Agha Mohammad Khan in the north, Jafar Khan retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, but local tribal chieftains such as Khosrow Khan and Mohammad Hosayn Khan Qaragozlu defeated him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader's return drove him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. In 1788 Agha Mohammad Khan came close to Shiraz, hoping to bait him out of its strong fortifications, but Jafar Khan stayed inside, and the Qajar leader went back to Isfahan, made his brother Ali Qoli its governor and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jafar Khan then raised another army and easily defeated the tribesmen Ali Qoli sent to Qumishah, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, but once more withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> when Agha Mohammad Khan hurried south. He was murdered on 23 January 1789 by Sayed Murad Khan, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-murad-khan-zand">Ali Murad Khan</a>. A civil war of four months among several Zand princes followed, from which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a> emerged victorious in May. His other sons included Khosrow Khan, Abbas Qoli Khan and Najaf Ali Khan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jafar Khan Zand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fifteen years of civil war followed, in which the throne passed in rapid succession between Sadeq Khan, Ali Murad Khan, <b>Ja'far Khan</b> and others, several of whom met violent deaths.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Malayer, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.29,48.82&amp;year=1779" aria-label="Malayer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5792 921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jafar Khan Zand"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6242 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6242 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.29,48.82&amp;year=1779">What was here in 1779? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
